<BlogArticle
	title="PAN Card Photo Requirements: Background, Dimensions, Dos and Don’ts"
	description="Prepare a PAN application photo with a clean background and accurate crop. Check current dimensions and file limits and follow these practical dos and don’ts."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/pan-photo-requirements/"
	heading="PAN card photo requirements: background color, dimensions, dos and don’ts"
	intro="A good source photo should be clear, recent, and composed as the application requests. Background color, aspect ratio, pixels, DPI, and file-size limits can vary by form and provider."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What to check in the live application</h2>
		<p>
			Before cropping, note the current form’s photo aspect ratio,
			pixel dimensions (if specified), resolution, accepted file
			format, maximum size, and background instructions. Indian
			applicants use the revised Forms 93/94 from 1 April 2026, so
			older PAN photo guides may describe a previous upload flow.
			Protean and UTIITSL requirements are not automatically
			interchangeable.
		</p>
		<p>
			Older reference presets often cite a 2.5 × 3.5 cm photo at 200
			DPI and 50 KB maximum for Protean/NSDL, or 213 × 213 pixels at
			300 DPI and under 30 KB for UTIITSL. Treat these as legacy
			references unless your current application displays them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Background color and framing</h2>
		<p>
			A plain, light or white background is a common PAN photo
			preparation recommendation because it separates the subject from
			the background. Use the color stated by the current application
			if it gives a specific instruction. Avoid patterned walls,
			clutter, visible objects, harsh shadows, glare, and strong
			backlighting. The face should be evenly lit and clearly
			recognizable.
		</p>
		<p>
			Frame the applicant straight-on and follow the form’s guidance
			for head position, face coverage, and shoulders. Leave enough
			space so the top of the head and chin are not clipped. Keep the
			crop’s original proportions; resizing must not stretch the face.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Do: prepare a clean, compliant image</h2>
		<ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Use a recent, sharp color photo with natural lighting and a neutral expression if the form asks for one.</li>
			<li>Remove distracting background space while keeping the subject correctly framed.</li>
			<li>Use the exact dimensions and aspect ratio shown in the live upload instructions.</li>
			<li>Save in the accepted format and check the resulting file size on your device.</li>
			<li>Open the exported copy and inspect it at full size before uploading.</li>
		</ul>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Don’t: rely on a preset alone</h2>
		<ul class="list-disc space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Do not assume the old 50 KB, 30 KB, or 213 × 213 pixel values apply to every current application.</li>
			<li>Do not use a blurry, low-light, overexposed, or heavily filtered image.</li>
			<li>Do not stretch a portrait into a square or use AI edits that change facial features or identity.</li>
			<li>Do not lower JPEG quality so far that facial details become blocky or smeared.</li>
			<li>Do not treat changing DPI metadata as a substitute for meeting pixel or physical-size requirements.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			A <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card photo resizer</a>
			can help crop and export an image, but it cannot validate an
			application form’s latest rules or guarantee approval. Check the
			current <a class="text-link" href="https://tinpan.proteantech.in/services/pan/pan-index.html">Protean PAN guidance</a>
			or <a class="text-link" href="https://www.pan.utiitsl.com/PAN/newA.html">UTIITSL application page</a>
			before submitting.
		</p>
	</section>
</BlogArticle>
